WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.71
This is journey five of Hewn, the software factory: raise the budget and resume.

00:00:06.71 --> 00:00:10.48
In journey four, a direction stopped itself at its budget.

00:00:10.48 --> 00:00:14.91
Now an Owner can give it more and send it on its way, in one action.

00:00:14.91 --> 00:00:20.05
This video walks through how it was built: the command, the resume, the dialog,

00:00:20.05 --> 00:00:21.75
the Viewer, and the tests.

00:00:22.90 --> 00:00:28.13
On a direction paused with Budget reached, an Owner presses Raise and resume,

00:00:28.13 --> 00:00:32.00
enters a new budget above the spend so far, and confirms.

00:00:32.00 --> 00:00:37.57
The budget is raised, and the direction resumes from its next step, in one action.

00:00:37.92 --> 00:00:43.28
A budget at or below the spend is refused inline, before anything changes.

00:00:43.28 --> 00:00:45.74
And Viewers never see the control.

00:00:45.74 --> 00:00:49.57
If they try the API anyway, it answers four oh three.

00:00:50.72 --> 00:00:52.60
Here is the whole exchange.

00:00:52.60 --> 00:00:58.40
The dialog posts the new budget, together with the budget the Owner was looking at.

00:00:58.40 --> 00:01:03.84
The API answers with a conflict, a validation error, or the resumed direction.

00:01:04.19 --> 00:01:06.98
The command first takes the project's lock,

00:01:06.98 --> 00:01:09.70
because model calls add to the same spend.

00:01:09.70 --> 00:01:14.63
If the budget is not the one the Owner saw, another session got there first,

00:01:14.63 --> 00:01:16.51
and the answer is a conflict.

00:01:16.51 --> 00:01:20.98
If the direction is not paused at its budget, that is a conflict too.

00:01:20.98 --> 00:01:26.16
And a new budget at or below the spend is refused, with a message for the field.

00:01:26.51 --> 00:01:31.15
Then, in one save, it records a Budget raised event with who raised it,

00:01:31.15 --> 00:01:35.86
hands the paused run to the pipeline, and publishes everything together.

00:01:35.86 --> 00:01:41.08
There is no moment where the budget is raised but the direction is still paused.

00:01:42.23 --> 00:01:44.53
Resuming reuses the run that paused.

00:01:44.53 --> 00:01:47.47
It goes back to the queue with its checkpoint,

00:01:47.47 --> 00:01:52.38
so the next worker to lease it carries on from the step after the last one it

00:01:52.38 --> 00:01:53.02
completed.

00:01:53.02 --> 00:01:56.91
The warning at eighty percent is re-armed for the new budget.

00:01:56.91 --> 00:02:02.20
And the stage, the direction and the project turn Running, each with its own event.

00:02:03.35 --> 00:02:07.03
The Raise budget dialog is built on the CDK dialog.

00:02:07.03 --> 00:02:12.93
It checks the amount before sending anything, sends the budget it was opened with,

00:02:12.93 --> 00:02:14.88
and closes with the result.

00:02:14.88 --> 00:02:17.90
A refusal from the API shows on the field.

00:02:18.25 --> 00:02:21.13
If another session raised the budget first,

00:02:21.13 --> 00:02:24.60
the dialog says the budget was not changed, and why.

00:02:24.60 --> 00:02:28.88
On a phone, the same dialog rises from the bottom of the screen.

00:02:30.03 --> 00:02:35.37
A Viewer sees the same pause, with what was spent, but no amount and no button.

00:02:35.37 --> 00:02:40.51
The end to end test signs in as a Viewer, checks that the control is absent,

00:02:40.51 --> 00:02:44.23
then posts to the API directly, and gets four oh three.

00:02:45.38 --> 00:02:48.04
Each slice began with its tests failing.

00:02:48.04 --> 00:02:52.94
The backend gained seven tests against the real API: the raise and resume,

00:02:52.94 --> 00:02:57.05
two budgets at or below the spend, the Viewer, a stale budget,

00:02:57.05 --> 00:03:01.29
a direction that is not paused, and another project's direction.

00:03:01.29 --> 00:03:05.07
All green, the backend has one hundred and seventy tests,

00:03:05.07 --> 00:03:08.32
and the worker one hundred and thirty, unchanged.

00:03:08.67 --> 00:03:11.43
The frontend has fifty three unit tests.

00:03:11.43 --> 00:03:16.06
End to end, a new pipeline test drives a real worker to the budget,

00:03:16.06 --> 00:03:20.75
refuses an amount, checks the Viewer and the dialog's accessibility,

00:03:20.75 --> 00:03:24.35
then raises the budget and watches the run carry on,

00:03:24.35 --> 00:03:26.97
for forty six end to end tests in all.

00:03:28.12 --> 00:03:32.79
Here is a direction with a ten dollar budget, paused at ten dollars twenty.

00:03:32.79 --> 00:03:36.34
The Owner types ten, and presses Raise budget and resume.

00:03:36.34 --> 00:03:40.88
Ten is not more than what was spent, so the dialog says so, on the field.

00:03:40.88 --> 00:03:41.50
Twenty is.

00:03:41.50 --> 00:03:44.61
The dialog closes, the direction is Running again,

00:03:44.61 --> 00:03:46.98
and the run picks up where it stopped.

00:03:46.98 --> 00:03:52.02
Spend climbs past ten dollars, live, the warning returns at eighty percent of the

00:03:52.02 --> 00:03:54.38
new budget, and the next stage begins.

00:03:54.73 --> 00:03:58.84
When spend reaches the new budget, the direction pauses again,

00:03:58.84 --> 00:04:02.28
with a fresh suggestion of one hundred dollars more.

00:04:02.28 --> 00:04:05.46
The same page works in dark mode and on a phone.

00:04:06.61 --> 00:04:08.32
Journey five is complete.

00:04:08.32 --> 00:04:12.35
An Owner raises a reached budget and resumes in one action.

00:04:12.35 --> 00:04:15.42
The direction carries on from its checkpoint,

00:04:15.42 --> 00:04:18.29
with spend and the warning starting again.

00:04:18.29 --> 00:04:23.82
And a budget at or below the spend, a stale budget, or a Viewer, are all refused.

00:04:23.82 --> 00:04:28.33
Next comes journey six: pause and resume a direction, at any time.
